<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seneca Falls Convention</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> Falls Convention was the first women's rights convention, held in the Wesleyan Chapel at Seneca Falls, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, on 19–20 July 1848. Organised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-cady-stanton">Elizabeth Cady Stanton</a> and local Quaker women during a visit by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretia-mott">Lucretia Mott</a>, it adopted a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-sentiments">Declaration of Sentiments</a> modelled on the Declaration of Independence, signed by 100 of some 300 people present. Its resolution on women's suffrage passed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass">Frederick Douglass</a> spoke for it. Stanton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susan-b-anthony">Susan B. Anthony</a> later presented it as the beginning of the women's rights movement, a view historians have since questioned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seneca-falls-convention.jpg" alt="Seneca Falls Convention" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woman's_Rights_Convention.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the decades before 1848 a small number of women pushed against the limits placed on them. Charles Grandison Finney allowed women to pray aloud in mixed gatherings from 1831, and from 1832 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-lloyd-garrison">William Lloyd Garrison</a>'s anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> associations encouraged women's full participation. The Grimké sisters and Abby Kelley spoke to mixed audiences against slavery, and Ernestine Rose lectured from 1836 on the enfranchisement of women. Under state statutes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a> women could not inherit property, sign contracts, serve on juries or vote, and they were paid about half of what men earned for the same work. On 7 April 1848 the New York State Assembly passed the Married Woman's Property Act.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The convention was planned on 9 July 1848 over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> at the Waterloo home of the Quaker Jane Hunt, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretia-mott">Lucretia Mott</a>, Mary Ann M'Clintock, Martha Coffin Wright, Jane Hunt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-cady-stanton">Elizabeth Cady Stanton</a>, the only non-Quaker, agreed to call a meeting while the Motts were in the area. The notice in the Seneca County Courier appeared on 11 July, giving eight days' warning. At the M'Clintock home on 16 July the women drafted ten resolutions and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-sentiments">Declaration of Sentiments</a>, which Stanton adapted from the Declaration of Independence, adding the phrase &quot;and women&quot; to &quot;all men … are created equal&quot;. Stanton added an eleventh resolution, on women's suffrage.</p><p>On 19 July the chapel doors were found locked, and Stanton's young nephew was lifted through a window to open them. About 40 men came although the first day had been announced for women only, and they were asked to stay silent. Stanton and Mott spoke and the declaration was read and amended. Mott gave an evening address to a large audience. On the second day James Mott chaired the morning meeting, since a woman chairing before men and women was still thought too radical. The declaration was adopted unanimously. Of about 300 present, 100 signed it, 68 women and 32 men. Many, Mott among them, urged dropping the suffrage resolution, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass">Frederick Douglass</a> argued for it and it was kept.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Other conventions soon followed, including one at Rochester two weeks later, and the first of a series of annual National Women's Rights Conventions met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a>, Massachusetts, in 1850. The historian Judith Wellman has called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-sentiments">Declaration of Sentiments</a> the single most important factor in spreading news of the movement in 1848 and afterwards. By the convention of 1851 women's suffrage had become a central tenet of the American women's rights movement. Contemporaries differed over the event: Mott saw it as one step among many, while Stanton considered it the beginning of the movement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seneca Falls Convention” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The status of Haudenosaunee women influenced the early American feminist movement around the <b>Seneca Falls Convention</b> of 1848.</blockquote><a href="/culture/haudenosaunee">Haudenosaunee · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Seneca Falls Convention</b> met in the Wesleyan Chapel at Seneca Falls, New York, on 19–20 July 1848.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>After the War of 1812 it gained a wider following, and from the 1820s variations of it were issued to proclaim the rights of workers, farmers and women, among them the <b>Seneca Falls Convention</b>'s Declaration of Sentiments of 1848.</blockquote><a href="/work/united-states-declaration-of-independence">United States Declaration of Independence · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
